WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.501
Fair warning.

2
00:00:01.521 --> 00:00:04.251
This is an embellished
fictional dramatization

3
00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:06.942
that may or may not be
based on true stories.

4
00:00:06.961 --> 00:00:09.632
The narrator is a guy
named Charlie Hack, and

5
00:00:09.652 --> 00:00:12.324
any resemblance to me
is mostly coincidental.

6
00:00:12.343 --> 00:00:16.828
Names, characters, companies and events
are fictitious, or used fictitiously.

7
00:00:16.848 --> 00:00:19.060
Silicon Valley C Players.

8
00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:22.449
This preview drops you into
the middle of the book.

9
00:00:22.469 --> 00:00:24.013
Part Three.

10
00:00:24.033 --> 00:00:27.076
It's called C Things That
Looked Like A Things.

11
00:00:27.096 --> 00:00:30.181
Silicon Valley is very good at
telling a story about A players.

12
00:00:30.201 --> 00:00:33.287
The people who look like they
were always going to win, and the

13
00:00:33.307 --> 00:00:36.004
companies that look like they
were always going to work.

14
00:00:36.024 --> 00:00:39.869
The myth is a straight line: a
garage, a genius, a ticker symbol.

15
00:00:39.889 --> 00:00:42.856
But most of the real work
looks nothing like that.

16
00:00:42.876 --> 00:00:46.780
It looks like another ordinary
Tuesday, doing the next right thing.

17
00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:50.996
And from the outside, a C
thing can look like an A thing.

18
00:00:51.016 --> 00:00:54.129
The applause is loud, and
the real work is quieter.

19
00:00:54.149 --> 00:00:59.068
This book is about that gap, between what
people saw and what was really going on.

20
00:00:59.088 --> 00:01:00.663
A C player is not a failure.

21
00:01:00.683 --> 00:01:05.670
It's the person who was never the smartest
in the room, and kept going anyway.

22
00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:08.586
Because there is always the
smarter guy in the room.

23
00:01:08.606 --> 00:01:11.337
The richer guy, the more
experienced, the wisest.

24
00:01:11.357 --> 00:01:15.628
The better father, the better brother,
the better sister, the better daughter.

25
00:01:15.648 --> 00:01:17.977
The Valley falls in love with products.

26
00:01:17.997 --> 00:01:19.974
Specific wins, in specific years.

27
00:01:19.994 --> 00:01:23.851
I got more out of building a
platform: a foundation you can stand

28
00:01:23.871 --> 00:01:27.316
on, then build the next thing
on, and the thing after that.

29
00:01:27.336 --> 00:01:29.552
You don't invent a career in one move.

30
00:01:29.572 --> 00:01:32.361
You stack building blocks:
habits, fundamentals,

31
00:01:32.381 --> 00:01:35.686
the unglamorous layer
nobody puts on a slide.

32
00:01:35.706 --> 00:01:38.380
That layer is what you
actually stand on later.

33
00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:43.636
It's all relative, how good
you look from the outside.

34
00:01:43.656 --> 00:01:46.304
But one thing stayed for certain.

35
00:01:46.324 --> 00:01:49.756
I just kept going. And
I kept doing my best.

36
00:01:49.776 --> 00:01:52.016
Then the work itself changed.

37
00:01:52.036 --> 00:01:55.190
I'm usually the most excited
person in the room about A.I.

38
00:01:55.210 --> 00:01:57.772
Which is funny, because
I should be the saddest.

39
00:01:57.792 --> 00:02:01.368
Because A.I. came after
the code writers first.

40
00:02:01.388 --> 00:02:05.445
In my own work, A.I. now writes
essentially all of the code I ship.

41
00:02:05.465 --> 00:02:08.262
That's how I work. Not
a measured team result.

42
00:02:06.603 --> 00:02:10.060
Not a general industry figure.

43
00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:13.043
Software engineering was
never about writing code.

44
00:02:13.063 --> 00:02:18.073
Writing code was just the tedious part
you had to do to build your ideas.

45
00:02:18.093 --> 00:02:20.997
It's about solving big
problems with abstraction.

46
00:02:21.017 --> 00:02:24.097
Each part is just data in,
some logic, and data out.

47
00:02:24.117 --> 00:02:25.266
Abstraction is key.

48
00:02:25.286 --> 00:02:28.132
You climb down the ladder,
and you climb back up.

49
00:02:28.152 --> 00:02:31.750
So software engineering practices
have to be geared around that.

50
00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:35.980
That's what Software Engineering 101
and 201 are about, and it's the second

51
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:39.820
half of this book: how to work now,
and how companies should use A.I.

52
00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:44.136
The book begins with a green-screen
dream, and it ends by dreaming about A.I.

53
00:02:44.156 --> 00:02:46.958
This part, in the middle, is the turn.

54
00:02:46.978 --> 00:02:48.175
It's free. It's open.

55
00:02:48.195 --> 00:02:50.942
And when version one is
ready, if you were around

56
00:02:50.962 --> 00:02:53.930
for any of it, you get to tell your side.

57
00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:55.756
The story is still being written.

58
00:02:55.776 --> 00:02:59.836
Silicon Valley C Players. Coming in 2027.
